now we've already talked about how the  NHS and business will cope with  coronavirus but it's also of course  putting pressure on other public  services like the police martin hewitt  the chair of the national police chiefs  council is helping to coordinate the  police response to the virus he joins us  now from Westminster thank you for being  on the program today  all the police ready for a crisis like  this well I think the first thing to say  Sofia is that right now police forces  across the UK officers and staff are out  there doing what we do they're in  communities keeping community safe they  are protecting and helping the  vulnerable and they're also dealing with  crime and criminality and we will  continue to do that but as you would  imagine and the word unprecedented has  been used a lot we are facing an  unprecedented situation but we've done  considerable planning we have always  planned to deal with pandemic among  other issues and working with those  other agencies that we will generally  operate with and we have our plans in  place both at a force level across the  UK and as you say I am coordinating now  a national effort to make sure that we  are able to cope with the the issues  that will come and face policing over  the coming weeks and months you say  those plans in place to do with the  pandemic I mean what kind of things  could we see I mean could we see police  for example at supermarkets or helping  assess the vulnerable so the first thing  that we are doing is obviously looking  at our own resourcing this virus is  indiscriminate and it will affect  policing so we've got we've got lots of  work going on individually enforces and  nationally to make sure that we are what  we're describing is orgonite  organizationally distancing so for  example where you have a control center  that answers 999 calls we've split those  into two and three in forces so we've  got distance and we're keeping those  services going and everything we're  doing is about keeping our operations  going we have written out in the last  week - there's over 10,000 special  constables around the country who  volunteer to help policing we are  talking to those special constables -  their employers about how they can  assist as we as we become challenged  around resourcing and as we wouldn't you  would expect we will be prioritizing how  we operate and we already prioritize on  a day to day basis in policing and  those things that represent the most  threats the most harm or risk and we  will continue to do that  the public are being I think are being  very cooperative at the moment and and  as we know people are starting to pay  much more attention to the measures that  are being asked to be to be adhered to  and I would really encourage everybody  to follow the guidance and the advice  that's coming out but what we are also  doing is is wanting people to come  through other channels to policing where  you can reduce the demand on policing  using digital methods using using other  component on 101 and coming in to us  when you need us but we stand ready to  do the role that the police undertakes I  keep people safe protect those  vulnerable people and then also to help  where we can help with the national  effort to defeat the virus you're  talking there a little bit earlier when  your answer about prioritizing I just  want to unpick what exactly that means  because of course the police would be  facing additional pressures because of  coronavirus does that mean that you are  going to have to prioritize and certain  lower level for example crimes may not  end up being investigated I think the  reality is we prioritize all the time  but we are in absolutely unprecedented  situations now our situation now and we  can also see that the the nature of  demand on police and the crime that's  committed will shift as everybody's  lives are changing in the way that they  are they are living their lives that  will shift and we are monitoring that  really closely to understand that shift  but we will have to prioritize as our  resources become more stretched which  they inevitably will we have to  prioritize those things that are the  most likely to cause harm or risk to  people and prioritize so that we are  able to to help the other services  particularly health social care services  local government to assist as we need to  assist in fighting the virus and what  kind of things would be deemed low  priority then well it's I don't really  want I don't think it's about Lowepro to  anything that is causing serious harm to  somebody will obviously be a priority  and policing is always prioritized we  are prioritized at the moment around  violence and serious violence we're  prioritized about where the most  vulnerable are in  to the pond so we are thinking really  carefully and have been thinking about  how we manage that prioritization in  these exceptional circumstances there's  been reporting that more people are more  vulnerable digitally in in this  environment where many people are at  home and we've been doing work through  our normal channels to put information  out to people about how they can prevent  themselves becoming vulnerable you will  have seen information that's coming out  from government coming out from the  National cybercrime Center so it's about  getting information out to people so  that they can protect themselves and  keep themselves safe and then us using  our resources as they inevitably become  stretched to do what we need to do  around those things that require require  a stronger police presence and require  us to protect people the the message  that I've been really struck from doing  this program talking to a range of  different guess is how important social  distancing is and how that is the one  thing that people believe could turn the  tide as the Prime Minister put on the  virus do you see if there are stricter  social distancing measures put in place  in the coming weeks that the police  could have a role to play in ensuring  some of these businesses stay shut  insuring pubs close and insuring people  perhaps don't go out unnecessarily so I  think the first point to make is that  the vast majority of of pubs clubs  businesses have been entirely compliant  with the messaging that came out on on  Friday and I think that's really  positive and and I will hope that that  would would carry on  of course as we move forward if there  are issues with people ignoring the  advice and this this is advice that is  saving people's lives  that's how serious it is if people are  ignoring that advice then clearly we  will play our part there are other  licensing authorities that will have a  role to play but of course the police  will play its part and when you talk  about people if we end up with with in  on the streets then I think the really  important point to make is we will carry  on doing our policing role and we will  carry on doing our policing role in the  way that we police in the in the UK  which is that traditional role of being  in communities policing with consent and  we will be doing whatever we do in  support of health officials this is a  health crisis  first and foremost and the really  important point is that  stop the level of transmission getting  higher and higher and where there is a  role for the police to play in that we  will play that role and obviously what I  will be doing is making sure that we are  able to do that across the UK and  equally importantly keeping our staff  safe because our officers and staff will  be out there in communities policing and  assisting with this effort and we've got  to do everything that we can do to keep  our staff safe because they are as  vulnerable as anybody else and clearly  they are as worried about their families  and their loved ones as everybody else  is of course I should say police staff  not completely invulnerable to  coronavirus which could put pressure on  numbers do you see a situation where you  could potentially working together with  the army so there's a range weak we  always have plans to be able to work  with not just the military with build  all sorts of other agencies that can  assist us we've put plans in place in  every force to manage how we get how we  survive and how we continue to deliver  our service if we have reduced numbers  particularly in specialist areas what my  teams are doing at the centre is we are  able as we already do for other big  issues we are able to move resources  around the country to support areas that  are particularly under pressure and I'm  getting daily reporting to understand  precisely where we are in terms of the  numbers of our officers and staff that  are unable to work either because  they're sick or they're self isolating  we will work with all parts of  government and we will provide we will  provide support and others will  undoubtedly provide support to us we  have well tested methods of working with  the military but I kind of restate the  point we are going to be capable of  policing in the way that we have policed  of course we will be stretched that's  why I ask people to think about placing  demands on our service but we are locked  in at a local level with all the other  emergency services and agencies that you  would expect and I'm locked in at a  national level so that we are able to  make the best use of our resources not  only to do our day job in policing but  also to support this extraordinary  national effort to to prevent the spread  of the virus  Martin Hewitt's thank you very much for  being on the program today thank you

